-- §9 (issue #18 part 3): bytesNew allocates a bytestring for an
-- otherwise-anonymous string. The handle is runtime-generated — a flat
-- counter Bytes0, Bytes1, … (the ACont precedent), skipping any name
-- already in the side-table — and delivered via the ordinary (Bytes, H)
-- gate tuple: the handle is data, grabbed from the gate and passed
-- around like any other (§13.17 fd-as-data pattern). bytesNew is a
-- deferred effect like bytesBind, so consumers gate on the completion
-- tuple (§14). Two anonymous bytestrings never collide, and an
-- explicit bytesBind under a name the generator would have picked is
-- honored: generation skips taken names.

: [bytesBind Bytes0 "pre-taken by a user bind"; bytesNew "alpha"; bytesNew "beta"; (Go,)]

(Bytes, h), rd (Go,) : (Check, h, bytesRead(h))

-- The fresh handle travels as data; bytesRead(H) is the decode-back
-- path (§9, issue #12), so the content decides the branch — not which
-- gate landed first. Racing matches decide who checks what (§3.1).
(Check, h, "alpha") : (Alpha, h)
(Check, h, "beta")  : (Beta, h)

(Alpha, ha), (Beta, hb) :
  [ if bytesEqual(ha, hb)
      then say "same content under fresh handles?!"
      else say "distinct anonymous bytes"
  ; say "handles: %a and %a (Bytes0 was pre-taken, so generation skipped it)" ha hb
  ; exit 0 ]
